You are an expert Python and React developer working on the Web Log Anomaly Detector project.

## Project
- Full-stack security tool: Python (FastAPI) backend + React (TypeScript) frontend
- Detects SQLi, XSS, path traversal, brute force, scanner agents in Apache/Nginx logs
- Backend parses logs into pandas DataFrames and runs 8 detection rules
- Frontend uses React 18, TypeScript, shadcn/ui, Tailwind CSS, and Vite

## Backend Rules
- All Python files follow PEP 8, formatted with Black
- Use pandas for all log data manipulation — never raw loops over rows
- FastAPI endpoints live in api.py only
- Detection rules live in detectors.py — each rule is an independent function
- Use type hints on every function
- Use f-strings, not .format() or %

## Frontend Rules
- All components use TypeScript — no implicit `any`
- Use shadcn/ui components first before writing custom UI
- Use Tailwind utility classes — no custom CSS files except index.css
- Components go in src/components/, pages go in src/pages/
- API calls go through src/lib/api.ts only — never call fetch() directly in components
- Use React hooks: useState, useCallback, useEffect — no class components

## API Contract
- Backend runs on http://localhost:8000
- POST /api/analyze returns: { summary: {...}, detections: [...] }
- Each detection has: { title, severity, count, data[] }
- Severity values are exactly: "HIGH", "MEDIUM", "LOW"

## Naming Conventions
- Python: snake_case for variables/functions, PascalCase for classes
- TypeScript: camelCase for variables/functions, PascalCase for components/types
- Files: kebab-case for Python modules, PascalCase for React components

## Do Not
- Never use `any` type in TypeScript
- Never call the backend directly from a component — use api.ts
- Never use inline styles in React — use Tailwind classes
- Never store uploaded log files on disk
- Never hardcode localhost URLs — use environment variables